-- §18 Every filed layer by how many of this repository's rules can say anything about it.
#
# ONE ROW PER LAYER, thinnest first, because the thin end is the whole point of the measurement.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ REPORTED, NEVER ASSERTED, AND THE THRESHOLD IS THE READER'S. `reports/coverage.sqlc`
# grades a RULE `thin` under three examined rows, and the mirror of that would be a rule about
# layers: *a layer under N rules is under-checked*. There is no N. A layer that files a demand,
# a nameplate, a quantum and five holders is reachable by more questions than one that files a
# nameplate and nothing else, and neither is a defect. What the number says is how much of this
# repository would have to be wrong before that layer's figures went unchallenged.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE ONE ROW WORTH LOOKING AT IS THE THIN END, AND IT IS NOT A VIOLATION. Measured, the
# minimum is 1: `every-absence/counter`, whose nameplate is `notApplicable`, so the vectors that
# most rules compose have no row for it and only a claim-grained rule reaches it. That is the
# corpus demonstrating its own boundary: a layer may be filed correctly, validate, and be
# almost entirely unrefutable, because a typed absence removes it from nearly every population.
#
# ⚠️ AND `violated = 0` EVERYWHERE IS THE CORPUS BEING CLEAN, NOT THE MEASUREMENT BEING BLIND.
# `examples/witnesses/main.rs` holds the smallest edit to a real document that makes each rule say
# no; run one and a layer's `violated` moves. That is the probe, and it is the difference
# between this column and a column nobody has ever seen change.
-- rank/layer_cover.sqlc, thinnest first.
SELECT c.filing        AS "filing!",
       c.layer         AS "layer!",
       c.examined_by   AS "examined_by!",
       c.violated      AS "violated!"
FROM (
    :compose(rank/layer_cover.sqlc)
) c
ORDER BY c.examined_by, c.filing, c.layer
